WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.730
As far as baseball movies go, Major League
was definitely a grand slam.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:09.650
The 1989 comedy featured a sweetheart season
for a down-and-out Cleveland Indians filled

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.950
with lovably bonkers characters, friction
between players and team executives, and a

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.450
lot of feel-good fun that still resonates
with audiences decades after the fact.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.010
Two sequels followed the original, and a third
has been knocking around the ballpark for

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.010
ages.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.220
Let's take a look back at the untold truth
of Major League.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.590
Winner takes all

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:31.770
Writer-director David Ward originally came
up with his concept for Major League based

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.710
on his own personal love for the ballclub
it featured.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:39.899
The Cleveland Indians, who again lost out
on a chance at the World Series title in 2016,

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:42.100
hadn't taken home a trophy since 1948.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.339
So, Ward, a longtime fan, wanted to turn their
luck around — even if it was just onscreen.

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:49.339
As he told Yahoo!

00:00:49.339 --> 00:00:53.620
Sports, "I started to feel like the only way
I would see the Indians win anything is if

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.280
I made a movie where they did.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.879
I realized it would have to be a comedy because
nobody would take this seriously."

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:00.879
Away game

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:05.480
Although the B-roll shots of Cleveland Municipal
Stadium were filmed in the Rock and Roll Capital

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.160
of the World, most of the movie was made in
another midwestern city: Milwaukee.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.400
Yes, although it might seem like a bit of
sports sacrilege for a movie about one ball

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.410
team to be shot on another's turf, much of
Major League was filmed at Milwaukee County

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.050
Stadium, which was home to the Brewers.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.750
Ward told ESPN that the reason for the shooting
locale shift was that the Cleveland Browns,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:30.080
the town's NFL team, was using the stadium
at the time for their pre-season games, so

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:31.840
it was unfit for filming.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.240
Talk about a foul ball.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.250
Performance enhancement

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:40.160
Given his torpedo-like public profile in recent
years, it might not seem too out of character

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.350
now for Charlie Sheen to have used performance-enhancing
drugs to beef up for his part in this movie.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.200
"Well, of course I'm on a drug.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.530
It's called Charlie Sheen."

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.860
What might be surprising, though, is why.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.110
Sheen told Sports Illustrated that he considers
the sport of baseball "not just a hobby [but]

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.460
a religion" and that it's the only sport he
cares about watching.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.370
His favorite team was actually the Indians'
rival, the Cincinnati Reds.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:06.200
Meanwhile, he even played baseball in high
school and was teetering on making it to the

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.800
big time at one point.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.409
Taking steroids, Sheen revealed, made his
fastball reach up to 85 miles per hour, but

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:16.939
it also made him a little testy when it came
to criticism of Ricky Vaughn's signature hairdo.

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:21.040
The actor admitted that because he was so
on edge from the drugs, he got into some scuffles

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.720
with bar patrons who made fun of his razed
mane during off-time from the shoot.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.160
Years later, people still consistently call
him "Wild Thing," — though, maybe for different

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.170
reasons these days — and he even revived
the character for Game 7 of the 2016 World

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:39.140
Series matchup between the Indians and the
Chicago Cubs in hopes of bringing a little

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.189
luck on his on-screen team.

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:42.189
Dream team

00:02:42.189 --> 00:02:46.319
While a lot of the actors had to attend baseball
camp to prepare for their turns around the

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:50.249
diamond, some of the cast members came to
the field with their own set of skills already

00:02:50.249 --> 00:02:51.249
groomed.

00:02:51.249 --> 00:02:54.740
Dennis Haysbert, for example, was such a good
hitter that he actually did knock it out of

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.810
the park with those insane homers we saw on
the big screen … and he didn't need Jobu

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.200
to do it, either.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.720
"I say —— you Jobu, I do it myself."

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.150
Wesley Snipes, on the other hand, was exactly
the opposite of his fast-running screen ego

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.170
WIllie Mays Hayes.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.730
He's said to have been terrible at throwing,
and couldn't nearly run as quickly as his

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.829
base-stealing counterpart.

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:16.859
Double switch

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:21.420
In Major League, the central villainess was
Rachel Phelps, the blithe team owner who purposefully

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.189
assembled the ragtag ensemble in hopes of
having them fail so she could move the organization

00:03:26.189 --> 00:03:27.230
to another site.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.909
"Here's to the thrill of defeat, Charlie."

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:34.200
In the end, the joke was on her once the team
came together to win their division and secured

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.000
their standing in Cleveland by selling out
seats at their hometown stadium.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.879
However, there was an alternate ending in
an early version of the movie that got booed

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:43.079
out of the theaters.

00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:47.110
Originally, Rachel was supposed to have a
scene that showed her as the not-evil mastermind

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.549
behind their runaway success who only acted
mean to encourage the budget squad to exceed

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:53.329
all expectations of them.

00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:57.180
Test audiences who saw the original cut, however,
didn't like the turnabout and said that they

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.519
wanted her to stay bad because they'd gotten
so used to hating her by the end.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:02.639
Free agent

00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:07.739
Long before he'd become a household name for
playing anxiety-addled agent Ari Gold in HBO's

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:11.610
Entourage, Jeremy Piven was chopped completely
out of Major League.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:15.809
Piven was originally expected to appear as
an insult-hurling benchwarmer for the team,

00:04:15.809 --> 00:04:18.170
but his scenes were left on the cutting room
floor.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:20.669
We can certainly imagine what that would be
like…

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:21.950
"Jesus Christ you all suck.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:25.960
If I didn't have a 5 year lease on this place
I'd shut it down and work out of a cardboard

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.440
box by myself!"

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.440
Double play

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.539
Although David Ward did return to helm the
1994 follow-up film Major League II, he doesn't

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.950
exactly consider the film to rank among his
finest achievements.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:41.240
He publicly panned the second pic, for which
he didn't write the screenplay, as being "not

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.569
as good as the first one" and said that it
"tried too hard to be funny."

00:04:44.569 --> 00:04:49.140
Neither Ward nor Charlie Sheen were involved
with the third installment, Back to the Minors.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.090
They both hated the movie so much that neither
of them even count it as a Major League movie

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:57.900
and widely refer to the sequel they're hoping
will happen next as Major League 3.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:58.900
The closer

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.531
Ward has written another installment to the
Major League movie-verse that's said to be

00:05:02.531 --> 00:05:07.849
set 20 years after the original events, after
Wild Thing has already retired from the sport.

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:12.199
The character would be brought back to train
a new generation phenom pitcher — who just

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:14.650
so happens to be his secret son.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.630
Sheen has said that he's fully on board with
the proposed project, that the script is definitely

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:23.210
winning and that it has only been delayed
on account of studio rain … for now.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:24.620
Thanks for watching!

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:27.580
